T/Siena, YouGov, Marist and Suffolk all show Acton ahead by four to nine points, and prediction markets price her as a clear favorite, supporting a small Democratic edge. Ohio's five-point Republican lean and Ramaswamy's roughly $30 million war chest keep the race well short of likely Democratic.", "watch": "Ramaswamy's spending advantage and late Republican consolidation could erase a lead of only a few points, especially if Acton struggles in the rural counties that have drifted from her party.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Mike DeWine (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Ohio's governorship is open for the first time in years, and Democrat Amy Acton enters the final stretch with a modest but consistent lead over Republican Vivek Ramaswamy. Surveys from the New York Times/Siena, YouGov, Marist and Suffolk all show Acton ahead by four to nine points, while the lone Republican-leaning pollster finds a one-point race. Prediction markets price her near a two-in-three favorite, and most race raters call it a tossup, with one calling it likely Democratic.\n\nThe race defies Ohio's recent Republican tilt: the state leans five points Republican at the presidential level, yet the 2026 environment has favored Democrats, and the open seat removes incumbent advantages. Ramaswamy holds a large financial edge, with roughly $30 million on hand to Acton's $8 million, giving him time to close. Acton's path runs through suburban areas and the rural counties that have drifted from her party.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.545, "margin": 0.49}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.5929, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.5919, "margin": 1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6166, "margin": 1.3}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5684, "margin": 1.2}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.592, "margin": 1.4}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6233, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6597, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-